// What the screens say about each weekly portion of Exodus (Shemot). The layout of each entry is
// explained at the top of lessons-genesis.js. Verse numbers follow the Hebrew Bible (as Sefaria does),
// which in a few chapters of Exodus differ by a few verses from English Bibles.
window.TORAH_LESSONS = window.TORAH_LESSONS || {};
Object.assign(window.TORAH_LESSONS, {

  'Shemot': {
    meaning: 'Names',
    summary: 'Jacob’s family grows into a people in Egypt, and a new Pharaoh enslaves them and orders every Hebrew baby boy thrown into the Nile. The midwives refuse. Moses is hidden in a basket, found by Pharaoh’s daughter, and raised in the palace. After killing an Egyptian who was beating a slave, he flees to Midian. At the burning bush God sends him back to free the Israelites, but at first Pharaoh only makes their work harder.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Brave people say no', text: 'The midwives Shifrah and Puah are ordered to kill every Hebrew baby boy. They quietly refuse, “because they feared God.” One of history’s first recorded acts of civil disobedience is two women saving babies.', source: 'Exodus 1:15–21' },
      { title: 'Don’t look away', text: 'Moses grows up a prince, but he “went out to his brothers and saw their burdens.” Rashi says he set his eyes and his heart to share their pain. Leadership starts with noticing other people’s suffering.', source: 'Exodus 2:11 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Turn aside and look', text: 'The bush burns but isn’t burnt up, and Moses says, “Let me turn aside and look at this marvelous sight.” Only when God sees him turn aside does God call his name. Paying attention comes before hearing a calling.', source: 'Exodus 3:3–4' },
      { title: '“I will be with you”', text: 'Moses asks, “Who am I to go to Pharaoh?” God doesn’t argue about his qualifications. God answers, “I will be with you.” You don’t have to feel ready. You just can’t go alone.', source: 'Exodus 3:11–12' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 1:10', headline: 'In Pharaoh’s council, keeping silent was its own kind of guilt.', text: '“Come, let us deal shrewdly with them,” Pharaoh tells his people, and the plan to destroy Israel begins in a meeting. The Talmud (Sotah 11a) imagines three advisers in that room. Balaam proposed killing the baby boys, and was later killed himself. Job said nothing, and was sentenced to the sufferings of the Book of Job. Jethro fled in protest, and his descendants sat on the Sanhedrin. The rabbis refuse to treat silence as neutral: the one who kept quiet ends up closer to the guilty than to the one who walked out.', try: 'Think of a meeting, group chat or family conversation where something wrong is going unchallenged. Say one clear sentence about it today, or step out.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 2:10', headline: 'Moses’ name for all time was given by Pharaoh’s daughter.', text: 'Pharaoh’s daughter knows exactly whose child she has found: “This is one of the Hebrew boys.” She defies her father’s decree, raises him, and names him Moshe, “because I drew him out of the water.” A midrash (Vayikra Rabbah 1:3) counts ten names Moses had and says God chose to call him only by this one, the name an Egyptian princess gave. It adds what God said to her: “Moses was not your son, and you called him your son. You are not My daughter, and I call you My daughter”: Bitya, “daughter of God.”', try: 'Think of someone outside your own family or people who rescued or shaped you. Thank them by name today, or tell someone else what they gave you.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 2:14', headline: 'Moses’ hardest discovery wasn’t Egypt’s cruelty but his own people’s.', text: 'The day after he kills the Egyptian, Moses tries to stop two Hebrews from fighting. “Who made you ruler over us?” one snaps. “Do you mean to kill me, as you killed the Egyptian?” Someone has talked. Rashi brings the midrash: Moses had wondered how Israel, of all nations, deserved such crushing slavery. “Now I know”: there were informers among them. The Torah doesn’t pretend the oppressed are saints. Moses runs for his life. Later he comes back to save the same people anyway.', try: 'Think of a group you belong to that has let you down up close: family, community, a team. Do one useful thing for it today anyway, the way Moses went back.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 3:2', headline: 'God chose a thornbush to say: I am in this pain too.', text: 'Of all the trees in the desert, God appears in a <i>sneh</i>, a scraggly thornbush. Rashi says why: “and not from any other tree,” because of the verse “I am with him in trouble” (Psalms 91:15). The midrash (Shemot Rabbah 2:5) goes further. God says to Moses: “Don’t you sense that I am suffering just as Israel is suffering?” The place itself is the message: “from the thorns,” God says, “I share in their suffering.” Before God shows power, God shows that the slaves are not suffering alone.', try: 'Sit with someone in their trouble today without trying to fix it or explain it. Just be in the thorns with them.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 4:6', headline: 'When Moses assumed the worst of his people, his hand turned leprous.', text: '“They won’t believe me,” Moses objects at the bush. God gives him signs: his staff turns into a snake, and his hand comes out of his cloak “leprous, like snow.” Rashi reads both as rebukes: he had slandered Israel, the serpent’s sin, and was struck with the skin disease Miriam would later get for slander. The Talmud (Shabbat 97a) is blunt: “One who suspects the innocent is struck in his body.” God tells Moses: they are believers, children of believers, and in the end it is you who won’t believe.', try: 'Pick one person you’ve quietly written off as unreliable. Give them a real chance today, as if you expected them to come through.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 4:24', headline: 'On his way to save Israel, Moses nearly dies at an inn.', text: 'One of the strangest scenes in the Torah: at a lodging place on the road to Egypt, God “sought to kill him.” Tzipporah grabs a flint, circumcises their son, and Moses is spared. Rashi, from the Talmud (Nedarim 31b), explains: Moses had a good reason to delay the circumcision while traveling, but when they reached the inn, “he busied himself with the lodging first.” Not neglect, just priorities slipping the moment he got comfortable. And it is his Midianite wife, not the prophet, who sees what must be done and does it.', try: 'Name the important thing you’ve been putting off until you’re “settled.” Do it tonight, before you unpack your evening.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 5:9', headline: 'Pharaoh’s cruelest weapon was keeping slaves too busy to think.', text: 'Moses asks for a few days to worship, and Pharaoh answers with a new policy: “Let the work be heavier on the men… so they won’t pay attention to empty words.” No more straw; the same quota of bricks. The Ramchal (<i>Mesillat Yesharim</i>, ch. 2) says Pharaoh meant to leave them no breathing room at all, to keep their hearts from any reflection. And he says the evil inclination still works this way, because it knows that if we stopped to look at our ways even briefly, we would start to change.', try: 'Take fifteen minutes today with no phone, no work and no noise, and ask yourself honestly what you would change about how you’re living.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vaera': {
    meaning: 'And I appeared',
    summary: 'God promises Moses to bring the Israelites out of Egypt, in four different words of rescue. Moses and Aaron confront Pharaoh, and Aaron’s staff turns into a serpent. Then come the first seven plagues: blood, frogs, lice, swarms of wild animals, disease of the livestock, boils, and hail. Each time, Pharaoh hardens his heart and refuses to let the people go.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Four promises, four cups', text: 'God promises: “I will bring you out, I will save you, I will redeem you, I will take you to be My people.” These four promises are why we drink four cups of wine at the Passover seder.', source: 'Exodus 6:6–7 · Jerusalem Talmud Pesachim 10:1' },
      { title: 'Too crushed to listen', text: 'The Israelites can’t listen to Moses “because of their crushed spirit and hard labor.” Exhausted people can’t take in good news. Help people catch their breath before you ask them to hope.', source: 'Exodus 6:9' },
      { title: 'Hard hearts get harder', text: 'Again and again Pharaoh hardens his heart. Each “no” makes the next one easier, until he can’t change his mind at all. Habits of the heart become who we are.', source: 'Exodus 7–9' },
      { title: 'Grateful even to water and dust', text: 'Aaron, not Moses, strikes the Nile and the dust. Rashi says Moses couldn’t hit the river that once saved his life, or the sand that hid him after he killed the Egyptian. Even things deserve our gratitude.', source: 'Exodus 7:19, 8:12 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 6:13', headline: 'Before the plagues, God orders Moses to treat Pharaoh with respect.', text: 'The Israelites are too crushed to listen, and Moses protests that if his own people won’t hear him, Pharaoh never will. Then: “God commanded them concerning the Israelites and concerning Pharaoh king of Egypt.” Commanded what? Rashi brings the midrash: toward Israel, to lead them gently and be patient with them; toward Pharaoh, to show him honor in everything they said. The king who has just made their labor crueler is still to be spoken to with honor. Moses must fight a tyrant without becoming coarse, and bear with a people who won’t listen.', try: 'With someone you’re in conflict with, or who won’t listen to you, keep every word respectful today, and be patient one more time.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 6:16', headline: 'Slavery couldn’t begin while one of Jacob’s sons was still alive.', text: 'In the middle of the drama the Torah pauses for a family list, and of Jacob’s sons named there, only Levi gets an age: 137 years. Why? Rashi, from Seder Olam, says it shows how long the slavery lasted, because as long as even one of Jacob’s sons was alive, there was no slavery, and Levi outlived them all. Only after “Joseph died, and all his brothers” does “a new king” arise. One living witness to who they were held Pharaoh back. When the last one died, the memory went with him.', try: 'Ask the oldest person in your family a question about where you come from, and write the answer down.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 7:3', headline: 'Pharaoh’s hardened heart was either his punishment or his last freedom.', text: '“I will harden Pharaoh’s heart,” God says before the plagues begin. How is that fair? Two great answers pull in opposite directions. Maimonides (Laws of Repentance 6:3): Pharaoh began the cruelty on his own, and a person can sin so much that the punishment is that repentance itself is withheld. The Sforno: God steadied Pharaoh’s heart so that pain alone wouldn’t break him; if he let Israel go, it should be real repentance, not collapse. One warns that a heart can practice “no” until “yes” is gone. The other says giving in because it hurts isn’t changing at all.', try: 'Name one habit you keep promising to quit “later.” Do the opposite of it once today, while change is still easy.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 7:15', headline: 'The god-king of Egypt snuck out at dawn to relieve himself.', text: 'God tells Moses to meet Pharaoh at the Nile in the morning: “he is going out to the water.” Why so early? Rashi, from the Midrash: Pharaoh claimed to be a god, and a god has no need to relieve himself, so he slipped down to the river at dawn to do it in secret. God sends Moses to the exact spot of the lie. And the first plague strikes the Nile itself, which Rashi says Egypt worshiped: their god is struck first. Power built on an image must keep hiding the body.', try: 'Name one image of yourself you work hard to keep up. Let one person see you without it today.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 8:14', headline: 'The magicians could fake rivers of blood, but not a louse.', text: 'Pharaoh’s magicians match the first two plagues: they too turn water to blood and bring up frogs. Then Aaron strikes the dust, and lice cover Egypt. The magicians try, “but they could not,” and they admit to Pharaoh: “This is the finger of God.” The Talmud (Sanhedrin 67b), quoted by Rashi, draws a strange rule: their dark arts had no power over anything smaller than a barley grain. The spectacular can be imitated. It is in the tiniest details that the fake gives out, and that is where they finally recognized God.', try: 'Look at the smallest details of your work or your word today, the ones nobody checks, and make them as honest as the big parts.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 8:22', headline: 'Pharaoh’s offer: worship your God, just don’t leave Egypt.', text: 'After the swarms, Pharaoh offers a deal: “Go, sacrifice to your God in the land.” Moses refuses. Their offering would be a sheep, and Rashi explains that the sheep was what the Egyptians worshiped; slaughtering it in front of them would get them stoned. Pharaoh tries again: go, “only don’t go very far.” Every offer keeps Israel within his reach. A freedom you may practice only inside your master’s borders, and only where it won’t offend him, isn’t freedom yet. Moses holds out for three days’ journey into the wilderness.', try: 'Notice one thing you believe in but only practice where it’s safe or convenient. Do it once today where it costs you something.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 9:20', headline: 'The Egyptians who believed Moses saved their horses for Pharaoh’s chariots.', text: 'Before the hail, Moses warns Egypt to bring everything in from the fields. Some of Pharaoh’s servants, the ones who “feared the word of the LORD,” rush their slaves and animals indoors; the rest lose theirs. Six plagues have taught them to take God seriously. But Rashi, from the Mekhilta, asks where Pharaoh found horses for the chariots that chase Israel to the sea, if Egypt’s livestock had died. His answer: from those who “feared the word of the LORD.” Believing enough to protect yourself is not the same as changing sides.', try: 'Think of a warning you took only far enough to protect yourself, from a doctor, a friend, your own conscience. Today act on the part that asks you to change, not just to take cover.' },
    ],
  },

  'Bo': {
    meaning: 'Come',
    summary: 'The last three plagues strike Egypt: locusts, darkness, and the death of the firstborn. God gives the Israelites their first commandments as a people: to set their calendar by the new moon, and to prepare the Passover lamb and mark their doorposts with its blood. At midnight the firstborn of Egypt die, and Pharaoh finally lets the people go. They leave in such a hurry that their bread has no time to rise.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Time is the first gift of freedom', text: 'The first commandment given to the Israelites as a people: “This month shall be for you the first of the months.” Free people own their time. Slaves don’t.', source: 'Exodus 12:2' },
      { title: 'Tell your children', text: 'Again and again the Torah says to explain the Exodus to children; the Haggadah’s four children come from these verses. A memory survives only if it’s passed down as a story.', source: 'Exodus 12:26, 13:8, 13:14' },
      { title: 'Light in the darkness', text: 'In the plague of darkness the Egyptians “could not see one another,” but “all the Israelites had light where they lived.” The worst darkness is when people stop seeing each other.', source: 'Exodus 10:23' },
      { title: 'Go before everything is ready', text: 'The Israelites leave so quickly that their dough doesn’t rise. That’s why we eat matzah on Passover. Freedom doesn’t wait for everything to be ready.', source: 'Exodus 12:34, 39' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 10:11', headline: 'Pharaoh thought worship was for men; Moses called it a family feast.', text: 'Before the locusts, Pharaoh offers a deal: the men may go and worship. Moses refuses: “We will go with our young and our old, with our sons and our daughters… for it is a festival of the LORD for us.” Pharaoh answers, “Go now, the men,” and Rashi spells out his logic: you asked to offer sacrifices, “and it is not the way of little children to offer sacrifice.” Pharaoh imagines religion as a duty adults perform. Moses describes a celebration a whole family shares. And Pharaoh knows that whoever holds the children holds the parents.', try: 'Bring someone much younger or much older into something you usually do alone: a blessing, a Shabbat meal, a walk to shul.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 10:22', headline: 'The Israelites who refused to leave Egypt were the comfortable ones.', text: 'For three days a darkness falls so thick that “no one could see his brother.” Why darkness? The Midrash (Shemot Rabbah 14:3) gives a painful answer: there were Israelites with Egyptian patrons, wealth and honor, who did not want to leave. They died in those days, and the darkness let them be buried unseen, so Egypt couldn’t say the Hebrews were struck just like them. Rashi later brings a view that only one in five left Egypt (13:18). The ones who stayed weren’t the most broken. They were the most comfortable.', try: 'Name one comfort that keeps you somewhere you know you shouldn’t stay: a job, a habit, a relationship. Ask yourself honestly what it is buying you, and what it is costing.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 10:26', headline: 'You don’t know what God will ask of you until you arrive.', text: 'After the darkness, Pharaoh makes his last offer: take the families, but leave the flocks. Moses won’t leave “a single hoof,” because “we will not know how we are to serve the LORD until we get there.” The Or HaChaim hears more than a question of how many sacrifices. God had asked for <i>avodah</i>, service, without saying what it would be; it might cost far more than animals. So they bring everything they own. Real commitment means taking all of yourself along before you know what it will be needed for.', try: 'Look at one commitment where you’re holding something back “just in case.” Bring it in fully today.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 12:6', headline: 'With no merits to be freed for, Israel was handed two commandments.', text: 'Why must each family set aside its lamb four days before Passover? The Mekhilta brings Rabbi Matya ben Charash: the time had come to keep God’s oath to Abraham, but the Israelites had no mitzvot of their own to be redeemed for. They were, in Ezekiel’s words, “naked and bare.” So God gave them two, the blood of the Passover lamb and the blood of circumcision, because “reward comes only through deeds.” Redemption was a gift, but not a passive one. Even people with nothing to show had to act with their own hands first.', try: 'Name one thing you’re waiting to deserve before you start: a relationship, a practice, a change. Do the first concrete act of it today, unworthy as you feel.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 12:27', headline: 'Israel bowed in thanks at the news their children would ask questions.', text: 'Moses teaches the elders the Passover rite and tells them: when your children ask you, “What is this service to you?”, you will answer them. “And the people bowed low.” Rashi says what they bowed for: the news of redemption, the promise of the land, and the news that they would have children. Slaves who had watched their sons thrown into the Nile hear that one day their children will question them, and they bow. The Haggadah gives that very question to the wicked son. They bowed for it anyway.', try: 'Ask someone younger what they really think about something that matters, or answer their hard question fully, even if it stings.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 12:32', headline: 'Pharaoh’s last words to Moses: “Bless me too.”', text: 'At midnight Egypt’s firstborn die, and Pharaoh, who once asked “Who is the LORD?”, calls Moses in the night: go, take your flocks, “and bless me too.” Rashi: pray for me that I don’t die, for I am a firstborn myself. Twice he has said “I have sinned,” each time to stop a plague. Now he asks for protection, not forgiveness, and never mentions the babies in the Nile. Within days he is chasing Israel to the sea. Fear can make a man ask for a blessing without changing anything.', try: 'Think of a time you apologized mainly to make the consequences stop. Today, tell that person plainly what you actually did.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 13:8', headline: 'We weren’t freed and then given rituals; we were freed for them.', text: '“You shall tell your child on that day: it is because of this that the LORD acted for me when I left Egypt.” Because of what? Rashi, from the Mekhilta: so that I would keep His commandments, like this Passover lamb, this matzah, this bitter herb. Not “we eat matzah because God freed us,” but “God freed us so that we would.” And the verse says “for me”: the Mishnah (Pesachim 10:5) rules from it that in every generation each person must see himself as if he personally left Egypt.', try: 'Take one practice you do out of habit and do it today as if it were the reason you were set free.' },
    ],
  },

  'Beshalach': {
    meaning: 'When he let go',
    summary: 'God leads the Israelites the long way, in a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night. Pharaoh chases them to the Sea of Reeds; the sea splits, Israel crosses on dry land, and the Egyptians drown. Moses and Miriam lead the people in song. In the desert the people complain about water and food, and God sends manna each morning, with a double portion before Shabbat. At Rephidim water comes from a rock, and Israel fights off an attack by Amalek.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Step into the water', text: 'Trapped at the sea, Moses cries out, and God says, “Why are you crying out to Me? Tell the people to go forward.” The Talmud says Nachshon walked into the sea before it split. Sometimes faith means moving first.', source: 'Exodus 14:15 · Sotah 37a' },
      { title: 'Sing', text: 'On the far shore, Moses and the people sing the Song at the Sea, and Miriam leads the women with drums and dancing. Saying thank you out loud, together, is part of being saved.', source: 'Exodus 15:1–21' },
      { title: 'Enough for today', text: 'Manna falls each morning, enough for that day; whatever is hoarded rots by morning. Trust that tomorrow will come with its own bread.', source: 'Exodus 16:16–20' },
      { title: 'Rest is a gift', text: 'On Friday a double portion of manna falls, and on Shabbat none. “See, the LORD has given you the Sabbath.” Rest isn’t something we have to earn. It’s given.', source: 'Exodus 16:22–30' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 13:19', headline: 'While everyone carried off gold, Moses went looking for a coffin.', text: 'On the night of the Exodus the Israelites are busy collecting the silver and gold of Egypt. Moses is busy elsewhere: “Moses took the bones of Joseph with him,” keeping an oath sworn generations earlier. The Talmud (Sotah 13a) holds it up: “Come and see how beloved the mitzvot were to Moses,” for “the wise of heart takes mitzvot.” And Moses didn’t know where Joseph lay. He had to ask the one old woman who still remembered, Serach daughter of Asher, who told him the coffin was sunk in the Nile.', try: 'On a day when everyone around you is chasing gain, keep an old promise you made to someone, especially one nobody will check.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 14:13', headline: 'At the sea, Israel panicked four different ways, and Moses answered each.', text: 'Egypt’s army behind them, the sea ahead. The Mekhilta says the people split into four groups: one wanted to throw themselves into the sea, one to go back to Egypt, one to fight, one to shout the enemy down. Moses’ words answer each in turn: “Stand still and see the LORD’s rescue”; “the Egyptians you see today you will never see again”; “the LORD will fight for you”; “and you, be still.” Despair, surrender, fighting and noise are how people panic. Moses asks for something harder: wait, and watch what is about to open.', try: 'The next time you panic today, notice which one you reach for: despair, retreat, a fight, or noise. Then wait one full minute before you act.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 14:20', headline: 'God silenced the angels’ song while the Egyptians drowned.', text: 'All night the cloud stands between the two camps, “and one did not come near the other all night.” Rabbi Yochanan (Megillah 10b) reads “one… the other” as the angels, who call “one to the other” in Isaiah’s vision. That night they wanted to sing, and God stopped them: “The work of My hands is drowning in the sea, and you would sing?” In the morning Israel will sing on the shore, and the Torah records their song. But God does not celebrate the death even of the enemy.', try: 'When someone you dislike gets what they deserve today, notice the satisfaction, and don’t add a single word to it.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 15:20', headline: 'The women had no time to bake bread, but they packed tambourines.', text: 'On the far shore “Miriam the prophetess took the tambourine in her hand, and all the women went out after her with tambourines and dancing.” Where did runaway slaves get drums? Rashi, from the Mekhilta: the righteous women of that generation were so sure God would do miracles for them that they brought tambourines out of Egypt. The dough had no time to rise, yet they made room for the instruments of a celebration that hadn’t happened yet. Faith here isn’t a feeling. It is something you pack.', try: 'Prepare today for a good thing you’re hoping for as if it’s already coming: buy the bottle, book the table, write the toast.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 16:7', headline: 'Abraham called himself dust; Moses said he wasn’t even that.', text: 'A month into the desert, the whole community turns on Moses and Aaron: in Egypt we ate bread until we were full. Moses doesn’t defend himself: “What are we, that you complain against us?” Your complaint, he says, is really against God. The Talmud (Chullin 89a) sets this beside Abraham’s “I am dust and ashes” and says Moses and Aaron went further: they weren’t even dust. And Rabbi Ile’a says the world stands on the merit of whoever holds back in a quarrel. Moses could absorb the anger because he wasn’t defending himself.', try: 'The next time someone complains to you today, don’t defend yourself. Ask what is actually hurting them.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 16:32', headline: 'Asked who’d pay the bills, Jeremiah held up a jar of manna.', text: 'Moses tells Aaron to keep a jar of manna “for your generations.” Rashi, from the Mekhilta, says when it was used: in Jeremiah’s day, the prophet rebuked the people for not studying Torah, and they answered, “Shall we leave our work to study? How will we make a living?” Jeremiah brought out the jar: “See the word of the LORD” (Jeremiah 2:31). This is what your ancestors lived on; God has many ways to feed those who revere Him. The excuse is ancient, and so is the answer.', try: 'Set aside thirty minutes this week, starting today, for learning that has nothing to do with earning, and guard it like a paid shift.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 17:12', headline: 'Moses refused a cushion while his people were fighting.', text: 'Joshua fights Amalek below; Moses holds up his hands on the hilltop. When his arms grow heavy, Aaron and Hur “took a stone and put it under him, and he sat on it.” The Talmud (Taanit 11a) asks: didn’t Moses have a pillow? He said: Israel is suffering, so I will be with them in their suffering. Rashi adds a sharper note: his hands grew heavy because he had sent someone else to fight in his place. Leading from the hilltop came with a duty to feel some of the pain.', try: 'Someone close to you is struggling right now. Give up one comfort today in solidarity with them, and let them know you’re with them.' },
    ],
  },

  'Yitro': {
    who: 'Jethro, Moses’ father-in-law',
    summary: 'Moses’ father-in-law Jethro, a Midianite priest, visits and celebrates all that God has done. Seeing Moses judge the people alone from morning to night, he advises him to appoint judges to share the load. The Israelites arrive at Mount Sinai and agree to God’s covenant. Amid thunder, lightning and the blast of a shofar, God speaks the Ten Commandments to the whole people.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Good advice can come from anyone', text: 'A Midianite priest, not an Israelite, gives Moses the advice that shapes Israel’s courts, and the portion with the Ten Commandments is named after him. Wisdom doesn’t check your membership card.', source: 'Exodus 18:13–24' },
      { title: 'Don’t burn out', text: '“What you are doing is not good. You will wear yourself out, you and the people with you.” Jethro tells Moses to share the work. Doing everything yourself isn’t devotion; it’s a mistake.', source: 'Exodus 18:17–18' },
      { title: 'One people, one heart', text: '“Israel camped there, opposite the mountain”: in the Hebrew, “he camped,” singular. Rashi says they were “like one person with one heart.” Unity, not sameness, prepared them to receive the Torah.', source: 'Exodus 19:2 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Ten words for living', text: 'The Ten Commandments begin with God and end with not wanting what belongs to your neighbor. How we relate to God and how we treat people come in one package.', source: 'Exodus 20:1–14' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 18:10', headline: 'It took a Midianite priest to say “Blessed is God” for Israel.', text: 'Jethro hears what God has done and comes out to the desert. He says: “Blessed is the LORD, who rescued you from the hand of Egypt.” The Talmud (Sanhedrin 94a) is stinging: “It is a disgrace for Moses and the six hundred thousand” that none of them said “Blessed” until Jethro came. And on the verse before, Shmuel reads Jethro’s joy, <i>vayichad</i>, as his flesh prickling: glad for Israel, he still felt Egypt’s fall. The outsider is the first to say “Blessed,” and he feels for the losers too.', try: 'Thank God, or a person, out loud today for something you’ve started taking for granted. Use the word “blessed” if you can.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 18:13', headline: 'One honest hour of judging makes you God’s partner in creation.', text: '“On the next day,” Moses sits judging the people “from morning until evening.” Rashi, from the Mekhilta and the Talmud, asks: all day? When did he study? Rather, any judge who rules truly, even for a single hour, is counted as if he studied Torah all day and became God’s partner in creation, which also ran “evening and morning.” In the Talmud (Shabbat 10a) this is taught to two rabbis who sat judging all day until they grew faint with hunger. The lesson was a kind one: one true hour counts, so go and eat.', try: 'Give one decision or dispute you’ve been avoiding a full, honest hour today. Then stop: you don’t have to carry it all day.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 18:25', headline: 'Even Moses couldn’t find judges with every quality Jethro asked for.', text: 'Jethro’s job description is demanding: capable people who fear God, who are trustworthy, and who hate bribes. But when Moses acts, the verse says only: “Moses chose capable men out of all Israel.” Retelling it forty years later, Moses says he asked for men “wise, understanding and known,” then appointed men “wise and known.” Rashi, from the Sifrei: “understanding men I could not find”; of all the qualities named, he found only three. Even Moses, choosing from a whole nation, couldn’t fill the ideal. He appointed the best real people he had and shared the load anyway.', try: 'Stop waiting for the perfect person to share a load you’re carrying. Hand part of it today to someone who is good enough.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 19:4', headline: 'God’s way of carrying you is to take the arrow first.', text: 'At Sinai, before a single commandment, God reminds them: “You saw what I did to Egypt, and how I carried you on eagles’ wings.” Rashi explains the image, from the Mekhilta. Other birds carry their young in their talons, afraid of bigger birds above. The eagle fears only the hunter’s arrow from below, so it carries its young on its back, saying, “Better the arrow strike me than my children.” Rashi adds that at the sea, the cloud caught Egypt’s arrows. Before asking anything of Israel, God describes love as standing between them and harm.', try: 'Take a hit for someone today: absorb the blame, the criticism or the cost that would otherwise land on someone more vulnerable than you.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 19:17', headline: 'Was Sinai a wedding or an ultimatum? Rashi reads it both ways.', text: 'Moses leads the people out “to meet God,” and they stand “at the bottom of the mountain.” Rashi offers two pictures. God comes out to meet them “like a bridegroom going out to meet his bride.” Or, from the Talmud (Shabbat 88a), the mountain is held over them like a barrel: accept the Torah, or this will be your grave. The Talmud admits this gives Israel a strong excuse, and answers that they accepted it again, freely, in the days of Purim. A covenant first received under pressure still has to be chosen later.', try: 'Take one obligation you inherited rather than chose, religious or otherwise, and decide today, freely, whether you choose it again.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 20:14', headline: 'Ibn Ezra’s cure for envy: a peasant doesn’t covet a princess.', text: '“Do not covet” sounds impossible: how can anyone be commanded how to feel? Ibn Ezra answers with a parable. A sensible peasant who sees a beautiful princess doesn’t desire her, because he knows she is simply out of reach, like wanting to grow wings and fly. A wise person sees his neighbor’s wife or wealth the same way: what God hasn’t given him, he can’t get by cleverness or scheming. Once that truly sinks in, what isn’t yours stops pulling at you. Envy isn’t beaten by willpower; it fades when you accept your portion.', try: 'Notice one person whose life, partner or success you keep measuring yourself against. Say to yourself plainly: that isn’t my portion. Then name what is.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 20:23', headline: 'If altar stones deserve dignity, how much more the person beside you.', text: 'Right after the Ten Commandments come rules for the altar: no steps up to it, only a ramp, “so that your nakedness is not exposed on it.” Rashi notes that the priests wore linen pants anyway; it is only about wide strides looking immodest. Then he draws the lesson, from the Mekhilta: if stones, which feel no shame, must not be treated with disrespect, then your fellow human being, made in your Creator’s image, who does feel shame, how much more so. Revelation is followed immediately by manners.', try: 'Watch for one moment today when you could embarrass someone, with a correction in front of others or a joke at their expense, and don’t.' },
    ],
  },

  'Mishpatim': {
    meaning: 'Laws',
    summary: 'Right after the Ten Commandments come dozens of practical laws: how to treat servants, what happens when someone is injured, lending money, returning lost property, protecting the stranger, the widow and the orphan, honest courts, Shabbat, and the three pilgrimage festivals. The people answer, “We will do and we will understand,” and Moses goes up the mountain for forty days and forty nights.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Holiness is in the details', text: 'Right after Sinai’s thunder, the Torah turns to damages, loans and lost oxen. Rashi says the portion opens with “and” to tie it to Sinai: these everyday laws are just as holy as the Ten Commandments.', source: 'Exodus 21:1 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'You were strangers too', text: '“Do not oppress the stranger, for you know how a stranger feels, having been strangers in the land of Egypt.” Our own pain is meant to make us kinder, not harder.', source: 'Exodus 23:9' },
      { title: 'Help even your enemy', text: 'If your enemy’s donkey has collapsed under its load, you must help him lift it. The Talmud says that when two people need help, help the one you dislike first, to conquer your own heart.', source: 'Exodus 23:5 · Bava Metzia 32b' },
      { title: 'Do first, understand later', text: 'The people say, <i>Na’aseh v’nishma</i>: “We will do, and we will understand.” Some things can only be understood by doing them.', source: 'Exodus 24:7' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 21:6', headline: 'The ear that heard “you are My servants” chose another master.', text: 'A Hebrew servant goes free after six years. But if he says, “I love my master… I will not go free,” his master pierces his ear against the door. Why the ear? Rashi brings Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai: the ear that heard at Sinai “the Israelites are servants to Me” went and acquired another master; let it be pierced. Why the door? Rabbi Shimon: the doors and doorposts saw God pass over them in Egypt and set Israel free. Now they witness a man choosing to stay a slave.', try: 'Name one “master” you keep serving although you could leave: a habit, an app, a job, a fear. Take one real step out the door today.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 22:3', headline: 'The Torah punishes the sneak more harshly than the armed robber.', text: 'A thief caught with stolen property “shall pay double.” Yet an armed robber who takes things openly only has to give back what he took. Rabban Yochanan ben Zakkai’s students asked him why (Bava Kamma 79b). He answered: the robber at least treats God and people alike; he fears no one. The thief hides from people but not from God, acting as if the Eye above doesn’t see and the Ear doesn’t hear. The heavier penalty falls on the one who cares more about being seen by people than by God.', try: 'Find one thing you do differently when nobody is watching, and today do it as if someone were.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 22:25', headline: 'Each night God holds your soul in pledge, then gives it back.', text: 'If you take your neighbor’s cloak as a pledge, “return it to him before the sun sets”: it may be his only blanket. Rashi brings a midrash (Tanchuma): God says, as it were, “How much you owe Me! Every night your soul goes up to Me, gives its account, and comes out in debt, and still I give it back to you every morning. So you too: take the pledge and give it back, take it and give it back.” Our mercy to debtors is meant to copy the mercy that wakes us up each morning.', try: 'Think of a debt or a grudge you’re holding over someone. Give it back today: forgive it, extend it, or simply stop bringing it up.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 23:1', headline: 'The Torah forbids listening to gossip, not only spreading it.', text: '“Do not carry a false report.” Rashi reads the Hebrew as “do not accept” one, following the Targum: it is a warning to the listener who takes in slander, and to a judge who hears one side before the other arrives. The Talmud (Pesachim 118a) notices the verse just before it: torn meat, “throw it to the dogs.” From there it says that whoever speaks slander, whoever accepts it, and whoever testifies falsely deserves to be thrown to the dogs. Gossip needs an audience, and the Torah holds the audience responsible.', try: 'The next time someone starts telling you something bad about a person who isn’t there, steer the conversation away, politely but for real.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 23:8', headline: 'A bribe works by making the giver feel like part of you.', text: '“Do not take a bribe, for a bribe blinds the clear-sighted.” The Talmud (Ketubot 105a) forbids even a bribe to rule correctly. Why? Rava explains (105b): once a judge takes something from one side, his mind draws close to that person until he is like himself, “and no one sees fault in himself.” The word <i>shochad</i>, bribe, is read as <i>shehu chad</i>, “he is one.” No money is needed: when a man gave Shmuel a hand off a ferry and then turned out to have a case before him, Shmuel disqualified himself.', try: 'Name one person whose favors make it hard for you to judge them honestly. Today, look at one thing they did as if a stranger had done it.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 23:20', headline: 'God’s promise of an angel was really a warning of distance.', text: 'Just after the laws, God promises: “I am sending an angel before you, to guard you on the way.” Rashi hears a warning in it: “Here they were told that they would sin,” and that God would one day say, “I will not go up in your midst” (33:3). After the golden calf, an angel instead of God’s own presence is exactly what is offered, and Moses refuses it: “If Your presence does not go, do not take us up from here.” A guardian is a gift. It can also be a sign that someone has stepped back.', try: 'Think of a relationship where you’ve settled for a stand-in: texts instead of visits, gifts instead of time. Today ask for, or offer, the real presence.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 24:10', headline: 'God remembers the bricks.', text: 'The elders see a vision of God with “something like a pavement of sapphire” beneath. Rashi says it was a brick, kept before God all through the slavery as a reminder of Israel’s suffering making bricks. Your hardest years are not forgotten.', try: 'Remember someone who’s struggling right now with a prayer or a call.' },
    ],
  },

  'Terumah': {
    meaning: 'Offering',
    summary: 'God asks the Israelites to bring gifts (gold, silver, copper, colored wool, wood, oil and spices) to build a Mishkan, a portable sanctuary, “so that I may dwell among them.” Detailed plans follow for the Ark of the Covenant with its two golden cherubim, the table for the bread, the seven-branched menorah, the curtains and boards of the Tabernacle, and the altar and courtyard.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Give from the heart', text: 'God asks for gifts “from every person whose heart moves him.” No one is taxed. A home for God is built out of what people choose to give.', source: 'Exodus 25:2' },
      { title: 'Everyone can build', text: 'Gold and silver, but also copper, goat hair and wood: rich and poor could all bring something. The Mishkan was built from many different gifts, big and small, and each one mattered.', source: 'Exodus 25:3–7' },
      { title: 'Gold inside and out', text: 'The ark is covered with gold inside and outside. The Talmud says a scholar whose inside isn’t like his outside isn’t really a scholar. Be the same person in private as in public.', source: 'Exodus 25:11 · Yoma 72b' },
      { title: 'Face each other', text: 'The two golden cherubim on the ark face each other, wings spread over the cover, and God’s voice comes from between them. Holiness lives in the space between people who turn toward each other.', source: 'Exodus 25:20–22' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 25:15', headline: 'The ark is the only vessel built never to settle down.', text: 'The table and the altars also have carrying poles, but only for the ark does the Torah command: “The poles shall stay in the rings of the ark; they shall not be removed from it.” The Sefer HaChinukh explains: the ark is the home of the Torah, so it must always be ready to go, if war or anything else forces a sudden move, with no hurried, careless lifting that might let it fall. He admits the ark itself is lost to us now. What it held never stopped traveling. The Torah’s home was built to leave.', try: 'Choose one practice you keep only at home, a blessing, a prayer, a Shabbat habit, and do it today somewhere else: at work, in the car, on the road.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 25:30', headline: 'In the Talmud, “always” can mean once each morning and night.', text: 'The bread of the Presence must lie on the table “always,” <i>tamid</i>. Yet Rabbi Yosei rules that if the old loaves came off in the morning and the new went on toward evening, that still counts as “always.” Rabbi Ami learns from him (Menachot 99b): one chapter in the morning and one at night fulfills “This Torah shall not depart from your mouth… day and night.” Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai says even the Shema does. Some say don’t tell that to the unlearned, lest they stop there; Rava says tell them, so they see what even a little is worth.', try: 'Pick one short piece of Torah, a verse, a paragraph, a Mishnah, and read it this morning and again tonight. Start today.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 25:31', headline: 'Moses received the Torah but couldn’t figure out a lamp.', text: 'The menorah must be hammered from a single block of gold, branches, cups and flowers all drawn out of one piece. Moses can’t picture it. The Talmud (Menachot 29a) lists three things so hard for Moses that God had to point them out “with His finger,” and the menorah is first. Rashi brings another midrash: Moses was so puzzled that God told him to throw the gold into the fire, and it came out made. The greatest prophet needed to be shown, more than once, and the Torah isn’t embarrassed to say so.', try: 'Ask for help today with something you feel you’re supposed to know already.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 26:15', headline: 'Jacob planted the sanctuary’s timber two centuries before it was needed.', text: 'Why does the Torah say “<i>the</i> boards,” as if they already existed? Rashi explains: Jacob had planted the trees in Egypt, and before he died he told his sons to take them along when they left, because God would one day command them to build a sanctuary in the desert. He planted at the beginning of the exile for the day it would end. Picture it: through every year of slavery those trees stood growing in Egypt, a promise anyone could see. The end of the story was rooted at its start.', try: 'Plant something today for a future you may not see: start a savings fund, write a letter to a child, teach someone a skill.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 26:33', headline: 'Behind the most guarded curtain, two golden figures were embracing.', text: 'The <i>parochet</i> curtain hides the Holy of Holies; only the High Priest goes in, and only on Yom Kippur. Yet the Talmud (Yoma 54a) brings Rav Ketina: when the people came up for the festivals, the priests would roll back the curtain and show them the cherubim, clinging to one another, and say: “See how you are loved before God, like the love of a man and a woman.” The Talmud struggles to explain how that was possible, and keeps it anyway. The innermost secret of the sanctuary was not a law or a weapon. It was an embrace.', try: 'Tell someone you love them today, plainly, without hiding it behind a joke or a favor.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 27:2', headline: 'The altar wore copper to atone for a brazen face.', text: 'The outer altar, where the offerings burn, is acacia wood overlaid with copper. Why copper? Rashi brings the Midrash Tanchuma: to atone for brazenness, the shameless face Isaiah calls a “forehead of copper” (Isaiah 48:4). The hardest sins to undo aren’t the ones done in weakness but the ones done with a straight face. The Talmud (Berakhot 12b) shows the way back: whoever commits a sin and is ashamed of it is forgiven all his sins. The altar of atonement is wrapped in the metal of the face that won’t blush.', try: 'Think of one thing you did that you defended instead of regretting. Let yourself feel the embarrassment today, and tell the person you’re sorry.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 27:10', headline: 'The courtyard’s curtains hung on hooks shaped like the word “and.”', text: 'Around the Mishkan runs a courtyard wall of linen hangings on sixty pillars. What holds the hangings up are hooks called <i>vavim</i>, and Rashi says each was made like the letter <i>vav</i>, one end bent up, the other driven into the pillar. In Hebrew, the letter <i>vav</i> placed before a word means “and”: it joins one thing to the next. And the hooks were silver, while the pillars stood in copper sockets: the connectors were the more precious metal. God’s courtyard hung not on its pillars alone but on its “ands.”', try: 'In one disagreement today, replace “but” with “and”: “I hear you, and I see it differently.”' },
    ],
  },

  'Tetzaveh': {
    meaning: 'You shall command',
    summary: 'The Israelites are to bring pure olive oil for a light that burns continually. God describes the clothes of the priests (Aaron’s apron-like ephod, a breastplate with twelve stones for the twelve tribes, a robe hemmed with bells and pomegranates, and a gold plate reading “Holy to the LORD”) and the seven-day ceremony to install Aaron and his sons as priests. It ends with the golden incense altar. Moses’ name doesn’t appear in this portion at all.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Keep the light burning', text: 'The <i>ner tamid</i>, the eternal light, must burn “continually.” Big moments are easy. Holiness is in keeping something going, day after day.', source: 'Exodus 27:20–21' },
      { title: 'Carry people with you', text: 'Aaron wears the names of the twelve tribes on his shoulders and over his heart whenever he goes before God. A leader carries the people with him, literally.', source: 'Exodus 28:12, 29' },
      { title: 'Dress for what matters', text: 'The priests’ clothes are made “for dignity and for beauty.” How we dress for something shows how much it matters to us.', source: 'Exodus 28:2' },
      { title: 'The missing name', text: 'This is the only portion since Moses’ birth that never mentions him by name. Tradition links it to his plea after the golden calf, “erase me from Your book” if You won’t forgive them. He stays present, unnamed.', source: 'Exodus 32:32 · Baal HaTurim' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 28:1', headline: 'Moses must personally ordain his brother into the job meant for him.', text: '“Bring near to you Aaron your brother… to serve Me as priest.” Rashi taught at the burning bush (4:14) that the priesthood was meant for Moses and went to Aaron when Moses kept refusing his mission. Now Moses must bring Aaron forward himself. The Midrash (Shemot Rabbah 37:4) says Moses was hurt. It compares him to a childless wife asked to choose her husband a second wife. God says: I could have made your brother High Priest without your knowing; I wanted you to be over him. And: “I had a Torah, and I gave it to you.”', try: 'Help someone succeed at something you once wanted for yourself: recommend them, introduce them, or celebrate them out loud today.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 28:30', headline: 'The same glowing letters could spell victory or woe.', text: 'Inside the folded breastplate, behind twelve stones engraved with the tribes’ names, Moses places the Urim and Tummim. The Ramban, following Rashi, says these were holy Names of God. When the priest asked a question, letters on the stones would light up before his eyes. But the letters that spell “Judah shall go up” can also spell “Lament over Judah.” Only the power of the Tummim gave the priest the inspired understanding to read them rightly. Even with God’s answer shining in front of you, the meaning still depends on how you read it.', try: 'Before you act on a message today, an email, a test result, a remark, write down two different ways to read it. Then ask before you decide which one is true.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 28:35', headline: 'The High Priest wore bells to atone for the sound of gossip.', text: 'Around the hem of the High Priest’s blue robe hang golden bells between woven pomegranates, “and its sound shall be heard when he enters the holy place.” The Talmud (Zevachim 88b) says each priestly garment atones for a different sin, and Rabbi Chanina explains the robe’s: let something that makes a sound atone for an evil sound, malicious speech. But no animal offering atones for slander. So the Talmud divides it: the incense, offered in private, atones for gossip whispered in private; the bells, for slander said out loud. The repair has to be as public as the harm.', try: 'If you once ran someone down in front of others, set it right today in front of the same people, or as close to them as you can get.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 29:1', headline: 'Aaron’s priesthood begins with an offering for the calf he made.', text: 'The seven-day ceremony that makes Aaron High Priest opens with an animal: “Take one young bull.” Rashi: a bull, to atone for the golden calf, since a calf is a young bull. The calf comes three chapters later, but Rashi holds the sanctuary was commanded after that sin. And Aaron is the one who made it. He doesn’t enter his new role by hiding his worst failure or waiting for people to forget it. The very first offering of his priesthood names it, in public, as part of the ceremony itself.', try: 'Before starting something new this week, tell someone honestly how you failed at it before and what you’ll do differently.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 29:20', headline: 'The new priest and the healed outcast receive the same three touches.', text: 'To consecrate the priests, Moses puts blood of the ram on the right earlobe, the right thumb and the right big toe of Aaron and his sons. The Torah uses this ritual for only one other person: someone healed of <i>tzara’at</i>, who has lived alone outside the camp, is touched in the same three places as the last step of his return (Leviticus 14:14). The holiest men in Israel and the man who had been sent away receive the same gesture. The Torah does not let a return be second-class. Coming back is its own kind of ordination.', try: 'If there is a place you’ve stayed away from out of embarrassment, a synagogue, a friendship, a family table, go back this week as someone who belongs there.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 29:46', headline: 'The Ramban’s “great secret”: God, too, needed to dwell with Israel.', text: 'God sums up the whole Exodus in one line: “I brought them out of the land of Egypt to dwell among them.” The Ramban accepts Ibn Ezra’s reading, that the dwelling was the very purpose of the Exodus, and says it holds “a great secret.” It would seem the Presence among Israel met a human need, “but it is not so”: it met a need above, as Isaiah says, “Israel, in whom I will be glorified.” The relationship isn’t charity from God to us. In a way beyond our understanding, God wanted it too.', try: 'Tell someone today that you need them, not only that they can count on you.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 30:7', headline: 'The holiest perfume needed one ingredient that stank.', text: 'Every morning and evening Aaron burns incense on the golden altar. Its recipe, given a few verses later, includes <i>chelbenah</i>, galbanum, a resin with a foul smell. The Talmud (Keritot 6b) notices that it doesn’t belong among sweet spices and draws a bold rule from it, in the name of Rabbi Shimon Chasida: “Any fast that does not include some of the sinners of Israel is no fast.” A community that prays only with its respectable members is missing an ingredient God asked for by name.', try: 'Invite to your next gathering, meal or prayer the person you’d rather leave off the list.' },
    ],
  },

  'Ki Tisa': {
    meaning: 'When you take a count',
    summary: 'Everyone gives a half-shekel for the census. Betzalel is chosen to build the Mishkan, and Shabbat is commanded again. While Moses is on Mount Sinai, the people make a golden calf. Moses smashes the tablets, then pleads for the people, and God forgives them. Moses asks to see God’s glory and is shown God’s back, with the Thirteen Attributes of Mercy. He brings down a second set of tablets, his face shining.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Everyone counts the same', text: 'Every person gives the same half-shekel for the census: “the rich shall not give more, and the poor shall not give less.” And each person gives only a half: it takes someone else to make a whole.', source: 'Exodus 30:15' },
      { title: 'Impatience leads astray', text: 'The people think Moses is late (Rashi says they miscounted the forty days) and panic. The golden calf comes from fear and impatience: the need for something they could see, right now.', source: 'Exodus 32:1 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Broken and whole together', text: 'Moses smashes the first tablets. The Talmud says the broken pieces were kept in the ark alongside the whole second set. Our broken parts have a place in the holy ark too.', source: 'Exodus 32:19 · Berakhot 8b' },
      { title: 'A God of second chances', text: 'After the golden calf, God is revealed as “compassionate and gracious, slow to anger, abounding in kindness and truth.” The Torah’s deepest description of God comes right after the people’s worst failure.', source: 'Exodus 34:6–7' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 31:16', headline: 'The verse commanding Shabbat teaches you to break it for a life.', text: '“The Israelites shall keep the Sabbath, making the Sabbath throughout their generations.” The Talmud (Yoma 85b) gathers proofs that saving a life overrides Shabbat, and this very verse is one. Rabbi Shimon ben Menasya: the Torah is saying, break one Shabbat for him, so that he will keep many Sabbaths. Another sage there reads “it is holy to you” to mean that Shabbat is handed over to you, not you to it. The holiest day of the week is never a trap a person may die in.', try: 'Name a rule you keep strictly. Ask what it is for, and whether today a person needs you to put them first.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 32:10', headline: 'When God says “leave Me alone,” Moses hears “hold on.”', text: 'On the mountain God tells Moses about the calf and says: “Now leave Me alone, so that My anger may blaze against them and I may destroy them.” Rashi notices that Moses hasn’t prayed yet, so why “leave Me alone”? God was opening a door, letting him know that it depended on him. The Talmud (Berakhot 32a) is bolder: Rabbi Abbahu says Moses grabbed hold of God the way a man grabs a friend by his coat, and said, “I won’t let go until You forgive them.” Sometimes “leave me alone” is an invitation to stay.', try: 'Argue today for someone whose case looks closed, to a boss, a family member, or in prayer, and don’t let go after the first no.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 33:13', headline: 'Moses asked why good people suffer, and may never have been told.', text: 'After the golden calf, Moses asks God: “Show me Your ways, so that I may know You.” The Talmud (Berakhot 7a) hears the oldest question in it: why do some righteous people prosper and others suffer, while some wicked suffer and others prosper? Rabbi Yochanan says God answered him, and the Talmud works through the answers. Rabbi Meir disagrees: this was the one request God refused. All Moses heard was “I will be gracious to whom I will be gracious,” even to the unworthy. The Talmud keeps both views and lets the question stand.', try: 'Think of a suffering you can’t explain, yours or someone else’s. Instead of forcing an answer today, do one gracious thing for someone in it.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 33:23', headline: 'Even Moses could know God only by what God leaves behind.', text: 'Moses asks, “Show me Your glory.” God answers that no one can see His face and live, but He will shelter Moses in a cleft of the rock: “you will see My back, but My face will not be seen.” Maimonides (<i>Guide for the Perplexed</i> I:38) reads “My back” as what follows from God: everything God has made and willed. The same Hebrew word, he notes, is used for walking “after” God, imitating His ways. God’s essence stays beyond even Moses. What anyone can know is the trail God leaves, and the way to know it is to follow it.', try: 'Pick one of the ways the Torah gives God a few verses later, “slow to anger, abounding in kindness,” and practice it on purpose today with someone who tests it.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 34:1', headline: 'Moses broke the tablets to save the marriage, not to end it.', text: '“Carve yourself two tablets like the first.” Rashi brings a parable from the Midrash. A king goes abroad, leaving his bride with her maids; their misconduct gives her a bad name. Her best man tears up the marriage contract, so that if the king means to punish her, he can say: she isn’t your wife yet. The king finds the maids were to blame and makes peace. Asked for a new contract, he says: you tore it, you bring the paper, and I will write it in My own hand. The best man is Moses; the bride is Israel.', try: 'In a relationship you damaged, bring the “blank stone” today: make the first concrete move, and leave room for the other person to write the rest.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 34:24', headline: 'The Talmud trusts a mitzvah to protect you, but not a miracle.', text: 'On the three pilgrimage festivals every Israelite man goes up to God’s house, leaving fields and flocks with no one to guard them. God promises: “No one will covet your land when you go up.” The Talmud (Pesachim 8b) learns that those on their way to a mitzvah come to no harm, going or returning. Then it sets a sober limit: where danger is common, you don’t rely on a miracle. Even Samuel, sent by God to anoint David, asked how he could go without Saul killing him. Faith may leave its fields unguarded. It doesn’t walk into fire.', try: 'Leave something you usually guard closely, your phone, your inbox, your work, for a few hours today for something that matters more.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 34:30', headline: 'After the calf, the people couldn’t bear even Moses’ shining face.', text: 'Moses comes down with the second tablets, his face radiant, and the people “were afraid to come near him.” Rashi, from the Sifrei: “Come and see how great is the power of sin!” Before the golden calf, they looked at God’s glory on the mountain, like a consuming fire, and didn’t flinch. After it, they shrank even from the light reflected on Moses’ face. Guilt doesn’t only make us afraid of punishment. It makes us afraid of goodness, and of anyone who reminds us who we were meant to be.', try: 'Is there someone you’ve been avoiding because they remind you of the person you meant to be? Call or write to them today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayakhel': {
    meaning: 'And he gathered',
    summary: 'Moses gathers the whole people, reminds them of Shabbat, and asks for gifts for the Mishkan. Men and women bring so much (gold, jewelry, yarn, wood, oil) that Moses has to tell them to stop. Betzalel and Oholiab lead the skilled craftspeople, and the work is done: the curtains, the boards, the ark, the table, the menorah, the incense altar, the copper altar, and the washbasin.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Rest comes first', text: 'Before building anything, Moses reminds the people about Shabbat. Even holy work stops for rest. The rabbis learn the 39 kinds of work not done on Shabbat from the work of building the Mishkan.', source: 'Exodus 35:1–3 · Shabbat 49b' },
      { title: 'Say when it’s enough', text: 'The people bring so much that the craftsmen tell Moses, “The people are bringing more than enough,” and he asks them to stop. Generosity overflowed, and enough was enough.', source: 'Exodus 36:5–7' },
      { title: 'Every craft can be holy', text: 'Skilled women spun the goat hair; others wove, carved and worked metal. Every kind of skill went into the holy place.', source: 'Exodus 35:25–35' },
      { title: 'What a crowd is for', text: '<i>Vayakhel</i> means “he gathered.” Moses gathers the same people who once gathered around Aaron to demand a golden calf. A crowd can build an idol or a sanctuary, depending on what it’s gathered for.', source: 'Exodus 35:1, 32:1' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 35:1', headline: 'The morning after their forgiveness, Moses gathered them to build.', text: '<i>Vayakhel</i>: Moses gathers the whole people. When? Rashi: “on the day after Yom Kippur, when he came down from the mountain” with the second tablets, after God forgave the calf. The first thing a forgiven people is asked to do is build a home for God. A later custom echoes it: the Rema writes that the meticulous begin building their sukkah right after Yom Kippur ends, going “from mitzvah to mitzvah.” Forgiveness isn’t the end of the story. It is where the building starts.', try: 'After your next apology or making-up, don’t just move on: start something small that you build together, today if you can.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 35:27', headline: 'The leaders waited to see what others gave, and lost a letter.', text: 'Everyone brings gifts for the Mishkan: brooches, yarn, skins, wood. The tribal princes come last, with onyx stones and gems. Rashi, from the Midrash, explains: the princes said, “Let the people give what they give, and we will make up whatever is missing.” But the people brought everything, and nothing was left for the princes but the stones. Because they were slow at first, a letter is missing from their title in this verse: <i>nesi’im</i> is written without its <i>yud</i>. At the altar’s dedication, Rashi adds, they made sure to give first.', try: 'Make the first offer today, the first pledge, apology or invitation, instead of waiting to see what others do.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 36:2', headline: 'No one had trained them; their hearts lifted them to try.', text: 'Moses calls “every wise-hearted person whose heart lifted him up to come to the work.” Where did slaves who made bricks learn to weave tapestries and set gems? The Ramban answers: they didn’t. None of them had learned these crafts from a teacher or trained their hands at all. Each one sensed in himself that he could do it, “his heart was lifted up in the ways of the LORD,” and he came to Moses and said, “I will do it.” The Mishkan was built by people brave enough to find out what they could do.', try: 'Volunteer today for something you’ve never been trained to do but suspect you could. Say “I’ll do it” before you feel ready.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 36:8', headline: 'The Torah tells the Mishkan five times because love repeats itself.', text: 'Now the Torah retells the Mishkan curtain by curtain, the same plans Terumah already gave. The Ramban counts five tellings in all and asks why. His answer: the repetition is “a sign of love and distinction,” showing that God desires this work and lingers over it, to increase the reward of all who study it. He compares the Midrash on Abraham’s servant, whose long retelling of his journey fills whole columns: “The ordinary talk of the patriarchs’ servants is dearer to God than the Torah of their children.” What God loves, God describes again.', try: 'Retell, in detail, something good a person you love once did, to them or to someone else, the way you’d tell a story you never tire of.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 37:1', headline: 'The ark needed the least skill and the deepest intention.', text: '“Betzalel made the ark.” Elsewhere the craftsmen work together; here the Torah names one man. Rashi: because Betzalel gave himself to this work more than the other sages, it is called by his name. The Ramban adds something surprising: making the ark took no great craftsmanship; other pieces were far harder. Betzalel made it himself because he was “filled with the spirit of God,” able to grasp what it meant and make it with the right intention. The holiest object called for the most attention, not the most technique.', try: 'Do one simple task today, a meal, a note, a blessing, with complete attention to what it means.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 37:29', headline: 'The incense makers’ brides never wore perfume.', text: 'Betzalel makes “the holy anointing oil and the pure incense, the work of a perfumer.” Centuries later, in the Temple, the House of Avtinas guarded the secret of making the incense smoke rise straight as a stick. The Talmud (Yoma 38a) faults them for keeping the secret, but praises them for this: no bride from their family ever went out perfumed, and a woman who married into it agreed not to wear perfume either, so no one could ever suspect them of using the holy incense for themselves. Integrity sometimes means giving up even what’s allowed.', try: 'Give up one thing today that’s permitted but could make someone question your honesty: a perk, a gift, a shortcut.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 38:8', headline: 'Moses called the women’s mirrors vanity; God called them the dearest gift.', text: 'The copper washbasin is made from the mirrors of the women who served at the Tent. Rashi, from the Tanchuma: Moses wanted to refuse them, as tools of vanity. God said: accept them, these are dearer to Me than everything else. In Egypt, when their husbands came home crushed by labor, the women brought them food, took out their mirrors, looked into them side by side and teased, “I’m more beautiful than you,” and woke their desire. That is how a nation of slaves kept having children. God called that holy.', try: 'Do something today to rekindle warmth or hope in someone worn down, a partner, a date, a friend: flirt, compliment, plan something to look forward to.' },
    ],
  },

  'Pekudei': {
    meaning: 'Accounts',
    summary: 'Moses gives a full public accounting of all the gold, silver and copper given for the Mishkan. The priests’ clothes are made, and all the finished parts are brought to Moses, who sees that everything was done exactly as commanded and blesses the people. On the first day of the first month the Mishkan is set up, and a cloud covers it as God’s glory fills it. The book of Exodus ends.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Be accountable', text: 'Even Moses gives a full accounting of every bit of gold and silver donated. People who handle other people’s money should be “clear before the LORD and before Israel.” Openness builds trust.', source: 'Exodus 38:21–31 · Numbers 32:22' },
      { title: 'Bless good work', text: 'When everything is finished, Moses sees they did it just as commanded, “and Moses blessed them.” Rashi says he prayed that God’s presence would rest on the work of their hands. Notice good work, and say so.', source: 'Exodus 39:43 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'From slavery to sanctuary', text: 'Exodus begins with the Israelites building cities for Pharaoh and ends with them building a home for God. Same hands, same skills. What changed is what they serve.', source: 'Exodus 1:11 · 40:33' },
      { title: 'Move with the cloud', text: '“When the cloud lifted, the Israelites would set out; when it stayed, they stayed.” They traveled on God’s timing, not their own. The book ends with them still on the journey.', source: 'Exodus 40:36–38' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Exodus 38:21', headline: 'The word for sanctuary hides the word for “pawned.”', text: '“These are the records of the Mishkan, the Mishkan of the Testimony.” Why say <i>Mishkan</i> twice? Rashi, from the Midrash Tanchuma: it hints at the two Temples, taken as a <i>mashkon</i>, a pledge, when they were destroyed for Israel’s sins. A pledge is held to be given back. Another midrash (Eichah Rabbah 4:14) finds mercy even in that: like a king, enraged at his son, who tore down the son’s wedding canopy instead of harming the son. God poured out His anger on wood and stones, and the people survived. The building was there to take the blow.', try: 'Today, let a thing take the hit instead of a person: lose the argument, pay the cost, cancel the plan, and protect the relationship.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 39:21', headline: 'The eighteen blessings of daily prayer echo the building of the Mishkan.', text: 'As each priestly garment is finished, the Torah closes with the same refrain: “as the LORD had commanded Moses.” Why does the weekday Amidah have eighteen blessings? One answer in the Jerusalem Talmud (Berakhot 4:3), in Rabbi Yochanan’s name: they match the eighteen times “commanded” appears in this second account of the building. When there is no sanctuary, prayer stands in its place, blessing by blessing, the way the Mishkan rose piece by piece. Each careful step of the building became a sentence of prayer.', try: 'Pray one blessing of the Amidah today slowly, word by word, as if you were setting a piece of the Mishkan in place.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 39:32', headline: 'A few craftsmen built the Mishkan; the Torah credits every Israelite.', text: '“All the work of the Mishkan was finished, and the Israelites did it, just as the LORD commanded Moses.” But Betzalel and the skilled workers did the work. The Sforno: the whole people is credited because each one had a share, by giving materials, labor or skill. The Or HaChaim goes further: the Torah can only be fulfilled by the whole nation together, each person doing commandments others cannot. That, he says, is “love your neighbor as yourself”: without the other, no one is whole. He also hears longing in “finished”: they would gladly have kept working.', try: 'Thank someone today whose part made something of yours possible, and name exactly what they contributed.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 39:33', headline: 'Moses built none of the Mishkan, so God let him raise it.', text: 'The craftsmen finish every piece, and then “they brought the Mishkan to Moses.” Why? Rashi, from the Tanchuma: no one could stand it up, because the boards were too heavy for human strength. And since Moses had done no work on the Mishkan, God left the raising for him. Moses asked how a person could possibly lift it. God said: work with your hand; it will look as if you’re raising it, and it will rise by itself. The man who had built nothing was given a part, and even then, the lifting wasn’t really his.', try: 'Find someone who has had no share in a project you’re part of, and give them a real piece of it today.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 40:2', headline: 'The Mishkan was raised and dismantled seven times before it stayed up.', text: 'God names the day: “On the first day of the first month you shall set up the Mishkan.” The Ramban, following the Sages, explains that this was the eighth day of the priests’ installation. On each of the seven days before it, Moses put the whole sanctuary up and took it down again. Why? Perhaps, the Ramban suggests, to train the Levites, who watched him and learned to do it themselves; or to crown the eighth day as the day it was raised and left standing. Holy things often need rehearsal before they stay.', try: 'Practice something important today that you’ll need to do well later, a hard conversation, a skill, a speech, even though it doesn’t count yet.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 40:17', headline: 'The Mishkan’s greatest day wore ten crowns and ended in grief.', text: '“In the first month of the second year, on the first of the month, the Mishkan was set up.” The Talmud (Shabbat 87b) says that day “took ten crowns,” among them: first day of the week and of the months, first day of priesthood and service, first fire on the altar, first priestly blessing, first day the Presence rested on Israel. Megillah 10b says God’s joy that day was like the day heaven and earth were created. And on that day Aaron’s sons Nadav and Avihu died. The Talmud keeps both on the same page.', try: 'If today holds both joy and sorrow for you or someone near you, don’t force it into one feeling. Name both.' },
      { verse: 'Exodus 40:35', headline: 'Exodus ends with Moses unable to enter God’s house.', text: 'The work is done. The cloud covers the Tent, and God’s glory fills the Mishkan so completely that “Moses could not enter the Tent of Meeting.” The man who spoke with God face to face stands outside. The next book opens: “He called to Moses.” The Ramban explains the link: Moses could not come near the place of the Presence except by being called in, just as at Sinai, where God “called to Moses from the cloud.” The book of building ends in waiting. Closeness, in the end, comes by invitation.', try: 'Today, where you’d normally push in with advice or an opinion, wait to be asked.' },
    ],
  },
});
